    "slug": "data-science",
    "title": "Data Science — From Foundations to Professional Practice",
    "description": "A visual-first, beginner-to-advanced course that preserves the attached 24-module, 258-lesson Data Science syllabus and progresses through the end-to-end workflow: define questions, acquire and prepare data, explore and communicate evidence, reason statistically, build and evaluate models, and operate solutions responsibly.",
    "category": "Data Science",
    "level": "Beginner to Advanced",
    "sort_order": 62,
    "version_scope": "Vendor-neutral foundations with documentation entry points checked on 2026-10-18. APIs, package versions, laws, standards, and platform instructions change; re-check official documentation before implementation.",
    "audience": [
      "beginners with basic computer literacy",
      "students and programmers learning applied data work",
      "analysts moving into statistics or machine learning",
      "professionals building reproducible and responsible data workflows"
    ],
    "prerequisites": [
      "No prior Data Science experience is required; basic computer literacy is sufficient.",
      "Python, SQL, mathematics, and statistics are introduced progressively.",
      "Advanced deep learning, distributed systems, and deployment work assumes earlier programming and data skills."
    ],
    "estimated_workload_hours": 220,
    "lesson_count": 258,
    "module_count": 24,
    "learning_outcomes": [
      "Frame a practical question and translate it into measurable analytical requirements.",
      "Collect, validate, clean, join, explore, and visualize data using reproducible workflows.",
      "Apply appropriate mathematical, statistical, inferential, and experimental methods and communicate uncertainty.",
      "Build, compare, and evaluate machine-learning methods against useful baselines without leakage.",
      "Explain specialist, time-series, text, deep-learning, and data-engineering approaches and their limitations.",
      "Document privacy, fairness, security, model monitoring, and operational risk throughout a data-science lifecycle."
    ],
